WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.933
- It's a warm, lovely day,

00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:16.766
and I'll try to keep us all
awake if I can (laughs).

00:00:16.766 --> 00:00:19.599
And our reward at the end of this,

00:00:20.721 --> 00:00:21.709
at the end of this lecture

00:00:21.709 --> 00:00:25.529
will be a reception to be held outside.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:27.529
So, let's rock and roll.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.226
Our topic tonight is
sustainable stewardship.

00:00:32.226 --> 00:00:35.471
And if we could have the lights off,

00:00:35.471 --> 00:00:38.064
it'll assist us all in
sleeping more soundly.

00:00:38.064 --> 00:00:39.290
(audience laughs)

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:43.938
And I'll be taking pictures
of those who nod off and

00:00:43.938 --> 00:00:47.215
(audience laughs)
posting it on the Internet.

00:00:47.215 --> 00:00:48.048
I got, uh.

00:00:51.162 --> 00:00:53.345
Before I actually get
into this talk, though,

00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:56.329
if you will indulge me
for five quick minutes,

00:00:56.329 --> 00:00:59.467
I know that there's folks here,

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:01.350
many of you that are involved in graphics,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.643
and in paper conservation, and
photographic-related matters.

00:01:05.643 --> 00:01:08.310
If I could just put that other hat on

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.810
for just a moment,

00:01:10.646 --> 00:01:13.479
I wanted to take just five minutes

00:01:14.515 --> 00:01:18.899
to familiarize you with
something that we are

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:21.996
very proud of at the
Image Permanence Institute

00:01:21.996 --> 00:01:25.449
that we have created as
a learning and teaching,

00:01:25.449 --> 00:01:27.782
primarily teaching, tool for

00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:32.233
the identification and understanding of

00:01:35.324 --> 00:01:38.365
graphics materials, images on paper.

00:01:38.365 --> 00:01:40.006
Starting back from wood-block prints

00:01:40.006 --> 00:01:44.173
and going forward to the
latest digital materials.

00:01:45.484 --> 00:01:48.026
And, in other words, it's process ID.

00:01:48.026 --> 00:01:50.494
It's a teaching tool for process ID.

00:01:50.494 --> 00:01:52.124
And it's to be found on the web at

00:01:52.124 --> 00:01:54.874
GraphicsAtlas, one word, dot org.

00:01:55.932 --> 00:01:58.182
And this is Graphics Atlas.

00:01:59.187 --> 00:02:03.183
And I'll give you just
the briefest tour of it.

00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:06.726
Everything on Graphics Atlas
is arranged around processes.

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:08.119
You see you got pre-photographic,

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:11.829
photomechanical,
photographic, and digital.

00:02:11.829 --> 00:02:13.943
And within each one, for example,

00:02:13.943 --> 00:02:16.056
within photographic processes,

00:02:16.056 --> 00:02:18.580
there are individual examples.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.250
And you can browse them by name

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.105
if you already know that you wanna,

00:02:24.105 --> 00:02:26.595
you wanna explore
chromolithograph, you can.

00:02:26.595 --> 00:02:29.653
Or there's a visual
browser, where you can say,

00:02:29.653 --> 00:02:32.957
"Does my thing look like this?"

00:02:32.957 --> 00:02:35.558
So, if your thing's a mezzotint,

00:02:35.558 --> 00:02:38.768
you think, yeah, okay,
let's explore the mezzotint.

00:02:38.768 --> 00:02:41.352
And that takes you to
one of the three ways

00:02:41.352 --> 00:02:43.565
to look at each individual material.

00:02:43.565 --> 00:02:45.842
This is called the guided tour.

00:02:45.842 --> 00:02:49.131
And the guided tour has selected images

00:02:49.131 --> 00:02:51.993
that tell you about this particular

00:02:51.993 --> 00:02:54.878
graphic object that you're looking at,

00:02:54.878 --> 00:02:58.838
and things to notice about
it, the prominent features.

00:02:58.838 --> 00:03:01.671
So you can click through them, and

00:03:02.706 --> 00:03:05.396
etched lines, the
characteristic rocker marks,

00:03:05.396 --> 00:03:08.418
and markings of the mezzotint, and so on.

00:03:08.418 --> 00:03:12.251
You can look at a little
video of the surface,

00:03:15.202 --> 00:03:16.369
when it works.

00:03:17.509 --> 00:03:19.426
Ah, yeah, there you go.

00:03:21.149 --> 00:03:24.524
All the materials, there's a
little video back and forth.

00:03:24.524 --> 00:03:27.693
Mezzotints are matt surface,
and have no reflections.

00:03:27.693 --> 00:03:30.178
So if it were glossy, you
would be able to see that,

00:03:30.178 --> 00:03:32.716
it's a bit analogous to
holding it in your hand.

00:03:32.716 --> 00:03:33.829
And there's a neat little thing here

00:03:33.829 --> 00:03:35.668
where you can click full-screen.

00:03:35.668 --> 00:03:38.009
All of those things,

00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:39.548
all of those views in the guided tour,

00:03:39.548 --> 00:03:43.660
you can click through one by
one and see it full-screen.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.910
So, that's the guided tour.

00:03:47.692 --> 00:03:51.859
And you can also do something
called compare view,

00:03:53.359 --> 00:03:56.482
where you can compare
one process to another.

00:03:56.482 --> 00:04:00.984
So, if you wanted to
compare a mezzotint with,

00:04:00.984 --> 00:04:04.932
oh I don't know, let's see, a cyanotype?

00:04:04.932 --> 00:04:06.727
(laughs)

00:04:06.727 --> 00:04:08.218
What you'll see is there's this,

00:04:08.218 --> 00:04:11.527
there's a dimension bar that shows you

00:04:11.527 --> 00:04:14.294
every image on this, and
you'll see that there are

00:04:14.294 --> 00:04:17.627
about 22 images of each process, can be,

00:04:18.749 --> 00:04:20.608
they're made in exactly the same way.

00:04:20.608 --> 00:04:24.281
So in the compare view, there's a little,

00:04:24.281 --> 00:04:26.081
there's a little driver's console here,

00:04:26.081 --> 00:04:27.265
where you can control whether you're

00:04:27.265 --> 00:04:29.307
looking at the front or the back.

00:04:29.307 --> 00:04:31.553
You can change the angle of light,

00:04:31.553 --> 00:04:32.947
so if you wanted raking light

00:04:32.947 --> 00:04:35.763
to see the texture, you can do that.

00:04:35.763 --> 00:04:38.419
You can do different magnifications.

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:41.759
And you can also see cross-sections

00:04:41.759 --> 00:04:43.435
labeled with each one.

00:04:43.435 --> 00:04:47.533
For example here in the, in the cyanotype,

00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:52.495
you see the ferricyanide
image in the paper fibers,

00:04:52.495 --> 00:04:55.095
and there's the ink on
the mezzotint, and so on.

00:04:55.095 --> 00:04:59.262
And there's a very neat
full-screen view for the primary

00:05:00.248 --> 00:05:01.081
thing, and there's,

00:05:01.081 --> 00:05:04.564
if you use the arrow keys
on your computer you can

00:05:04.564 --> 00:05:05.731
cycle through.

00:05:07.251 --> 00:05:09.280
Have to be on the surface.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.401
You can cycle through
all the magnifications

00:05:11.401 --> 00:05:13.228
and all the views,

00:05:13.228 --> 00:05:16.144
and change whether you're looking at it

00:05:16.144 --> 00:05:19.346
from a 45 degree angle,
from a zero degree angle,

00:05:19.346 --> 00:05:20.679
and from a 90 degree angle.

00:05:20.679 --> 00:05:24.775
So it's a pretty, it's an
interesting virtual print shop,

00:05:24.775 --> 00:05:27.187
or a virtual lab where you could study

00:05:27.187 --> 00:05:30.514
prints under the microscope and really

00:05:30.514 --> 00:05:33.817
really begin to explore
their characteristics.

00:05:33.817 --> 00:05:37.984
And the third view in here
is called identification.

00:05:39.127 --> 00:05:41.894
That's a single page that, um,

00:05:41.894 --> 00:05:43.561
takes you through...

00:05:44.604 --> 00:05:45.961
Hmm.

00:05:45.961 --> 00:05:48.794
This is not Graphics Atlas's fault.

00:05:48.794 --> 00:05:49.757
(audience laughs)

00:05:49.757 --> 00:05:52.007
But what we will do is, um,

00:05:53.789 --> 00:05:58.453
start from home in case it
is Graphics Atlas's fault.

00:05:58.453 --> 00:06:02.400
So if you wanted to find
out how to identify a,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.755
for example, an inkjet print,

00:06:04.755 --> 00:06:05.993
you can do that here.

00:06:05.993 --> 00:06:07.957
So there's a single page telling you

00:06:07.957 --> 00:06:11.418
all about the key
characteristics of inkjet prints.

00:06:11.418 --> 00:06:13.220
Different dot structures
that they might have,

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:15.698
different back printing.

00:06:15.698 --> 00:06:19.541
There's your little video of
this particular inkjet print,

00:06:19.541 --> 00:06:22.736
and talking about abrasion, and so on, so.

00:06:22.736 --> 00:06:26.020
As some of you may know
Bamber Gascoigne's book,

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:27.944
How to Identify Prints.

00:06:27.944 --> 00:06:30.939
This is a kind of electronic
version of that very thing,

00:06:30.939 --> 00:06:32.786
and I think it's very good
for classroom teaching,

00:06:32.786 --> 00:06:33.982
and so on, so.

00:06:33.982 --> 00:06:37.625
Thank you for indulging
me on that commercial.

00:06:37.625 --> 00:06:39.125
GraphicsAtlas.org.

00:06:42.412 --> 00:06:44.662
And now, to the main event.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:47.990
Sustainable stewardship.

00:06:49.105 --> 00:06:50.804
The subtitle is really the important one,

00:06:50.804 --> 00:06:54.574
it's talking about the new
thinking on preservation

00:06:54.574 --> 00:06:57.180
environments and building operations.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:01.272
Are there any building
operators in the audience?

00:07:01.272 --> 00:07:02.105
Any bites?

00:07:03.155 --> 00:07:05.331
Anybody wants to, you know, be singled out

00:07:05.331 --> 00:07:07.331
for building operations?

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:09.561
- [Audience Member] Sort of.

00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:10.926
- [James] Sort of?

00:07:10.926 --> 00:07:14.309
(laughs) Well, everyone,
everyone has a house,

00:07:14.309 --> 00:07:17.314
so they're building
operators for that, but.

00:07:17.314 --> 00:07:19.314
Anyway, let's plunge on.

00:07:20.938 --> 00:07:22.630
This is what Bobby told you about us

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:24.742
at the Image Permanence Institute.

00:07:24.742 --> 00:07:28.011
And let me take the opportunity
to acknowledge the sponsors.

00:07:28.011 --> 00:07:31.261
NEH, IMLS, Andrew W. Mellon Foundation,

00:07:32.772 --> 00:07:35.796
and the Society for Imaging
Science and Technology.

00:07:35.796 --> 00:07:37.564
They are the people that have made

00:07:37.564 --> 00:07:40.980
IPI and all of its work possible.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:44.526
What I'm gonna try to do
in the next little while is

00:07:44.526 --> 00:07:47.753
talk about some of the ideas that have

00:07:47.753 --> 00:07:52.223
become current in our work
and the work of others.

00:07:52.223 --> 00:07:54.942
Concepts, talk about some
of the tools and approaches

00:07:54.942 --> 00:07:57.844
to managing environments, and

00:07:57.844 --> 00:08:00.528
focus in on a couple of projects that

00:08:00.528 --> 00:08:03.024
we have been involved in.

00:08:03.024 --> 00:08:05.739
Major ones at the Library of Congress.

00:08:05.739 --> 00:08:07.528
There's a project that we're embarking on

00:08:07.528 --> 00:08:11.445
here at Yale University
that is funded by IMLS.

00:08:13.211 --> 00:08:16.042
In that project we will be

00:08:16.042 --> 00:08:19.150
exploring the concept of saving energy

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:23.317
by shutting off air handlers
during unoccupied hours

00:08:24.495 --> 00:08:26.785
in library stack spaces.

00:08:26.785 --> 00:08:31.346
And our sandbox for this
particular fun day at the beach

00:08:31.346 --> 00:08:34.793
will be the stacks here
in Sterling Library,

00:08:34.793 --> 00:08:38.407
which are served by six
different air handling units.

00:08:38.407 --> 00:08:41.126
We'll be having fun with that.

00:08:41.126 --> 00:08:43.219
I will also tell you
about a similar experiment

00:08:43.219 --> 00:08:46.073
that we have begun at
the Library of Congress.

00:08:46.073 --> 00:08:49.016
And then I wanted to share with you

00:08:49.016 --> 00:08:51.348
the opportunity to take a look at some

00:08:51.348 --> 00:08:53.373
new museum storage buildings

00:08:53.373 --> 00:08:55.477
that are extremely low energy

00:08:55.477 --> 00:08:58.144
that have been built in Denmark.

00:08:59.193 --> 00:09:01.297
I could go on about them
for a very long time,

00:09:01.297 --> 00:09:03.130
but we'll get to that.

00:09:04.329 --> 00:09:06.713
Every, here's a little,

00:09:06.713 --> 00:09:11.123
part of this is gonna be a
primer on this whole subject.

00:09:11.123 --> 00:09:13.314
I recently had the opportunity to

00:09:13.314 --> 00:09:17.601
visit 10 historically black
colleges and universities

00:09:17.601 --> 00:09:20.929
to consult with them on
the environments in their

00:09:20.929 --> 00:09:24.596
special collections
departments of their libraries.

00:09:24.596 --> 00:09:27.883
And this is the library at
Prairie View A&amp;M University

00:09:27.883 --> 00:09:30.133
in suburban Houston, Texas.

00:09:31.604 --> 00:09:32.880
It's a nice new building.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.927
It's got great collections,
was built in the 1980s.

00:09:36.927 --> 00:09:40.626
And this view has a little
symbolic thing going on

00:09:40.626 --> 00:09:43.722
because one of the things
that you need to know

00:09:43.722 --> 00:09:45.937
about this whole subject is that

00:09:45.937 --> 00:09:49.770
the outside climate is
hugely determinative of

00:09:50.927 --> 00:09:52.523
what the inside climate is like,

00:09:52.523 --> 00:09:55.675
what are the possibilities
for saving energy?

00:09:55.675 --> 00:09:57.254
And the two things that combine

00:09:57.254 --> 00:10:01.421
to create the inside climate
are the outside climate

00:10:02.371 --> 00:10:06.288
and the mechanical systems
inside the building.

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:12.155
Just pause to review some of
the real fundamentals, here.

00:10:12.155 --> 00:10:14.405
When you build a structure,

00:10:15.335 --> 00:10:18.826
and have systems to control
the climate inside it,

00:10:18.826 --> 00:10:21.785
it's fairly easy to control temperature.

00:10:21.785 --> 00:10:25.149
The machines to make cold and to make heat

00:10:25.149 --> 00:10:28.112
are easy to build and easy to control.

00:10:28.112 --> 00:10:30.977
The tough job is controlling moisture.

00:10:30.977 --> 00:10:32.492
And that's what we want to do in library,

00:10:32.492 --> 00:10:34.906
and archive, and museum environments

00:10:34.906 --> 00:10:38.214
is we want control over both
temperature and moisture.

00:10:38.214 --> 00:10:42.381
And it's the moisture part
that's really the difficult one.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:47.093
And here is where the complexity
with the outdoor climate

00:10:47.093 --> 00:10:48.426
comes into play.

00:10:50.278 --> 00:10:53.435
We pay a lot of attention
in environmental management

00:10:53.435 --> 00:10:55.795
to this parameter called dew point.

00:10:55.795 --> 00:10:57.328
Now dew point is the temperature at which

00:10:57.328 --> 00:10:59.957
moisture will condense out of the air.

00:10:59.957 --> 00:11:03.124
But dew point is also a direct measure

00:11:04.794 --> 00:11:07.842
of the amount of moisture in air.

00:11:07.842 --> 00:11:10.950
And since there's often
too little moisture

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:13.605
in the outside air or too much
moisture in the outside air,

00:11:13.605 --> 00:11:17.000
depending on the season, what you,

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.957
how much outside air that
comes into your building,

00:11:18.957 --> 00:11:22.950
and whether it's the right
quantity of moisture,

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:24.202
it has the right dew point,

00:11:24.202 --> 00:11:27.369
that's where the details get devilish.

00:11:28.981 --> 00:11:31.924
And the more outside air,
here's a little energy note,

00:11:31.924 --> 00:11:36.603
just in general, you save
energy by not running machinery.

00:11:36.603 --> 00:11:39.703
And when you're bringing
in more outside air,

00:11:39.703 --> 00:11:41.678
and especially when, in
the heart of the summer,

00:11:41.678 --> 00:11:44.761
or the heart of the winter, when it's

00:11:46.059 --> 00:11:50.059
too cold and too dry, or
too warm and too moist,

00:11:50.982 --> 00:11:54.535
that's when you spend a lot of money.

00:11:54.535 --> 00:11:57.884
The more work you work you
have to do on outside air,

00:11:57.884 --> 00:12:00.253
the worse it is for energy.

00:12:00.253 --> 00:12:02.267
Well, as far as the
collection is concerned,

00:12:02.267 --> 00:12:04.342
and we're back again,
here, at Prairie View A&amp;M,

00:12:04.342 --> 00:12:05.682
in that building.

00:12:05.682 --> 00:12:07.337
If you go down to the
basement of that building,

00:12:07.337 --> 00:12:09.376
that's where you see machines like this

00:12:09.376 --> 00:12:12.352
that make the environment
inside the building.

00:12:12.352 --> 00:12:14.662
This is what's called
an air handling unit.

00:12:14.662 --> 00:12:16.462
And what you're looking at is,

00:12:16.462 --> 00:12:19.706
it's a big sheet-metal box,
there's a fan the middle,

00:12:19.706 --> 00:12:23.216
straddling each side, in
this particular case, is,

00:12:23.216 --> 00:12:27.374
are these pipes, and behind
and inside the machine is

00:12:27.374 --> 00:12:30.838
a cooling coil, and a
heating coil, and a fan.

00:12:30.838 --> 00:12:33.388
Those are the real fundamental elements.

00:12:33.388 --> 00:12:35.467
And it's this machine's job

00:12:35.467 --> 00:12:37.298
to provide a human-comfort environment,

00:12:37.298 --> 00:12:41.465
and to provide a suitable
environment for the collections.

00:12:43.576 --> 00:12:45.421
This is a rare sight.

00:12:45.421 --> 00:12:46.449
I'm gonna show you one of the

00:12:46.449 --> 00:12:49.032
rarest sights you've ever seen.

00:12:50.578 --> 00:12:54.346
There you see a meeting of the minds

00:12:54.346 --> 00:12:58.513
between the library director
and the building operator,

00:12:59.585 --> 00:13:02.418
together, in the mechanical rooms.

00:13:03.719 --> 00:13:05.291
And you're gonna hear from me that

00:13:05.291 --> 00:13:09.371
the thesis of my work
is that the preservation

00:13:09.371 --> 00:13:13.538
of the collection depends hugely
on the storage conditions.

00:13:14.789 --> 00:13:19.178
I could talk 'til you are
all bleeding at the eardrums

00:13:19.178 --> 00:13:22.345
about the ways in which (laughs)

00:13:22.345 --> 00:13:23.963
temperature and humidity conditions

00:13:23.963 --> 00:13:26.167
really do affect the conditions.

00:13:26.167 --> 00:13:28.291
But if you'll simply grant me that,

00:13:28.291 --> 00:13:30.536
if the jury will stipulate that,

00:13:30.536 --> 00:13:34.052
we can go on to talking
about the other stuff, okay?

00:13:34.052 --> 00:13:36.111
And so here you see,

00:13:36.111 --> 00:13:39.941
here you see what ideally has to happen.

00:13:39.941 --> 00:13:43.274
My thesis will be that we can no longer,

00:13:44.244 --> 00:13:49.128
if we're preservation or
we're in the collections part

00:13:49.128 --> 00:13:53.133
of a cultural institution,
we can no longer

00:13:53.133 --> 00:13:57.066
be an effective advocate for
the health of our collections

00:13:57.066 --> 00:14:01.273
in this way, unless we have a
kind of meeting of the minds.

00:14:01.273 --> 00:14:03.482
And it was really funny, the
fellow there had worked there

00:14:03.482 --> 00:14:06.679
for 11 years, that'd be
the guy in the blue shirt.

00:14:06.679 --> 00:14:08.012
He is explaining

00:14:10.085 --> 00:14:11.752
a panel of controls.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:16.332
The machine I just showed
you, the air handler?

00:14:16.332 --> 00:14:20.082
That is controlled via
a computerized system,

00:14:21.912 --> 00:14:24.619
and that's like the little junction box

00:14:24.619 --> 00:14:27.444
where all the wires that are connected

00:14:27.444 --> 00:14:29.672
to individual valves and dampers

00:14:29.672 --> 00:14:32.728
go to sort of a concentration point,

00:14:32.728 --> 00:14:35.260
and from there they
are sent to a computer.

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:38.639
So this fellow can operate
this library's systems

00:14:38.639 --> 00:14:41.259
from his office, which was across campus,

00:14:41.259 --> 00:14:45.272
or anyone in the whole
Johnson Controls organization

00:14:45.272 --> 00:14:47.978
could be thousands of miles away,

00:14:47.978 --> 00:14:50.226
can see what's going on and operate it.

00:14:50.226 --> 00:14:51.805
This is what's called a BMS,

00:14:51.805 --> 00:14:53.877
or a building management system.

00:14:53.877 --> 00:14:56.868
Sometimes called an
energy management system.

00:14:56.868 --> 00:14:59.521
The funny part about getting
everybody together to see this,

00:14:59.521 --> 00:15:02.874
I insisted that the library
directors and the archivists

00:15:02.874 --> 00:15:05.568
go on a tour of the mechanical rooms.

00:15:05.568 --> 00:15:09.172
And this was what's so rare about this,

00:15:09.172 --> 00:15:12.391
at one moment I said to the
guy here in the blue shirt,

00:15:12.391 --> 00:15:15.891
I said, "Wow, I guess this is the biggest,

00:15:17.693 --> 00:15:19.848
biggest crowd you've ever had down here?"

00:15:19.848 --> 00:15:21.787
And he said, "This is the only crowd

00:15:21.787 --> 00:15:24.342
"I've ever had down here."
(audience laughs)

00:15:24.342 --> 00:15:25.850
And it was,

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:27.731
I did this 10 times.

00:15:27.731 --> 00:15:29.782
And sometimes I had to drag
these library directors

00:15:29.782 --> 00:15:33.731
kind of kicking and screaming
through the mechanical rooms.

00:15:33.731 --> 00:15:35.691
But it's extremely instructive.

00:15:35.691 --> 00:15:37.551
It concretizes what's going on,

00:15:37.551 --> 00:15:41.777
and it embodies this idea,
embodies the whole thing.

00:15:41.777 --> 00:15:43.043
Now, one of the things you'll hear me say

00:15:43.043 --> 00:15:47.418
in just a moment is how
complex these systems are.

00:15:47.418 --> 00:15:49.617
And I explain that there,
you know, that there was

00:15:49.617 --> 00:15:53.664
a computer somewhere where
you could see and visualize

00:15:53.664 --> 00:15:57.254
the moving parts of this, the
building management system.

00:15:57.254 --> 00:16:01.836
This is what one part of such
a system would look like.

00:16:01.836 --> 00:16:03.552
This is the schematic part,

00:16:03.552 --> 00:16:06.689
where every moving part is represented.

00:16:06.689 --> 00:16:11.637
And if you look closely,
there are little values there.

00:16:11.637 --> 00:16:14.856
This is what the operator of a building

00:16:14.856 --> 00:16:19.790
sits at a computer terminal
and looks at screens like that.

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:23.414
Alright, now let's talk
about sustainability

00:16:23.414 --> 00:16:26.747
and really get to the fundamentals here.

00:16:28.108 --> 00:16:30.202
Environments cost a lot of money.

00:16:30.202 --> 00:16:31.350
They burn a lot of energy.

00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:34.367
And in many cases that's
fossil fuel energy.

00:16:34.367 --> 00:16:36.223
And as a rule of thumb, you can think

00:16:36.223 --> 00:16:37.909
for every square foot of temperature

00:16:37.909 --> 00:16:40.433
and humidity-controlled space,

00:16:40.433 --> 00:16:44.202
an average cost per year,
just for the energy,

00:16:44.202 --> 00:16:46.515
not for the salary of the blue shirt guy,

00:16:46.515 --> 00:16:50.299
not for the amortized
capital cost of building it,

00:16:50.299 --> 00:16:53.381
but just the amount of electric
and gas it burns each year,

00:16:53.381 --> 00:16:56.214
is $4 per square foot, on average.

00:16:57.931 --> 00:17:00.117
For example, the University
of California Los Angeles

00:17:00.117 --> 00:17:04.310
has 850,000 square feet of library stacks,

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:06.185
so you begin to do the math

00:17:06.185 --> 00:17:10.036
and you're talking about big big money.

00:17:10.036 --> 00:17:11.373
I just showed you an example of

00:17:11.373 --> 00:17:13.484
a building management system.

00:17:13.484 --> 00:17:18.028
One of the reasons why it's
so rare that preservation,

00:17:18.028 --> 00:17:21.774
collections, staff like
librarians and so on,

00:17:21.774 --> 00:17:26.251
and facilities staff and
building operators get together,

00:17:26.251 --> 00:17:30.340
is everybody's job is
very very complicated.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:32.227
And from the building side,

00:17:32.227 --> 00:17:34.827
the buildings are really
hard to understand,

00:17:34.827 --> 00:17:38.462
they're very individualistic,
there are many generations

00:17:38.462 --> 00:17:40.692
of those building management systems,

00:17:40.692 --> 00:17:43.662
there's band-aids on top of band-aids,

00:17:43.662 --> 00:17:46.467
there are dumb systems
designed from the beginning.

00:17:46.467 --> 00:17:49.276
There are all kinds of reasons why

00:17:49.276 --> 00:17:52.047
understanding them does not come easily.

00:17:52.047 --> 00:17:54.792
And no one should underestimate

00:17:54.792 --> 00:17:57.859
the reality that it's difficult.

00:17:57.859 --> 00:18:00.160
Now, I asked you to stipulate to me

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:03.393
that environments do
matter for the collections.

00:18:03.393 --> 00:18:06.278
And the points that are important

00:18:06.278 --> 00:18:10.267
in the sustainability context for that are

00:18:10.267 --> 00:18:13.275
we've spent a long time, I'm at,

00:18:13.275 --> 00:18:15.891
more or less, at the
tail end of my career.

00:18:15.891 --> 00:18:19.065
But I've spent at least 30 years

00:18:19.065 --> 00:18:20.428
studying the ways in which

00:18:20.428 --> 00:18:22.439
materials deteriorate in the laboratory.

00:18:22.439 --> 00:18:25.348
Causing them to deteriorate
through accelerated aging.

00:18:25.348 --> 00:18:27.304
Reading about deterioration.

00:18:27.304 --> 00:18:30.196
Going to conferences,
listening to people talk about

00:18:30.196 --> 00:18:32.019
the ways in which collections are

00:18:32.019 --> 00:18:34.986
harmed or helped by the environment.

00:18:34.986 --> 00:18:38.133
And, what's the net result of all of that?

00:18:38.133 --> 00:18:39.883
I can say to you that

00:18:40.966 --> 00:18:43.591
most professionals that are
in the preservation field,

00:18:43.591 --> 00:18:47.651
or in the curatorial or librarian field

00:18:47.651 --> 00:18:51.484
would give less latitude
for safe environments

00:18:52.335 --> 00:18:55.071
for collections than I think is real,

00:18:55.071 --> 00:18:56.994
than I think the science would support.

00:18:56.994 --> 00:18:59.247
In other words, there is more latitude

00:18:59.247 --> 00:19:02.156
to do different things
with the environment.

00:19:02.156 --> 00:19:05.443
And I'll speak to that
point a little bit later.

00:19:05.443 --> 00:19:07.711
But here's a crucial point, as well.

00:19:07.711 --> 00:19:10.042
And that is, suppose we wanted to

00:19:10.042 --> 00:19:12.087
save some of that big money,

00:19:12.087 --> 00:19:15.005
some of the money we're
spending to condition

00:19:15.005 --> 00:19:18.680
to precise temperature and
humidity all those square foot

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:22.847
of libraries, of museum
stores, of archival materials.

00:19:25.343 --> 00:19:27.077
How are we gonna do that?

00:19:27.077 --> 00:19:30.528
What's gonna be the problem
standing in the way?

00:19:30.528 --> 00:19:33.376
What's gonna be in the
way is not the ability to

00:19:33.376 --> 00:19:38.247
turn things off, because that's
three clicks on the mouse.

00:19:38.247 --> 00:19:40.974
You can shut it off
with three mouse clicks

00:19:40.974 --> 00:19:42.820
and the password.

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:45.397
That's the easy part, and
that'll save you money.

00:19:45.397 --> 00:19:47.452
The hard part is knowing
when to do that safely

00:19:47.452 --> 00:19:49.883
and responsibly for your collections.

00:19:49.883 --> 00:19:52.233
And who's gonna be
prepared to answer that?

00:19:52.233 --> 00:19:54.299
Is it gonna be the facilities person?

00:19:54.299 --> 00:19:55.132
No.

00:19:55.132 --> 00:19:56.162
Is it gonna be the CFO?

00:19:56.162 --> 00:19:57.087
No.

00:19:57.087 --> 00:19:59.724
Is it gonna be the director, or the dean,

00:19:59.724 --> 00:20:02.992
or the chief curator, or whoever?

00:20:02.992 --> 00:20:05.325
No, it's got to be the person who has

00:20:05.325 --> 00:20:07.524
the collection's care responsibility.

00:20:07.524 --> 00:20:11.237
Be they conservator, preservation
administrator, or so on.

00:20:11.237 --> 00:20:14.066
Their guidance is critical.

00:20:14.066 --> 00:20:17.006
Their input is critical to achieving

00:20:17.006 --> 00:20:20.503
a sustainable condition in
these cutural institutions.

00:20:20.503 --> 00:20:24.287
In other words, it ain't
on those buildings guys.

00:20:24.287 --> 00:20:25.900
It ain't on the CFO.

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:30.112
It's on the preservation
and conservation function.

00:20:30.112 --> 00:20:33.943
And they have got to step
up, and they are the ones

00:20:33.943 --> 00:20:36.387
who are gonna describe this envelope

00:20:36.387 --> 00:20:40.496
of reasonable safe operation
for the collections.

00:20:40.496 --> 00:20:42.496
It might even be better.

00:20:45.024 --> 00:20:46.304
Big money?

00:20:46.304 --> 00:20:48.218
Well, you be the judge.

00:20:48.218 --> 00:20:52.207
This is a woman that's an archivist at

00:20:52.207 --> 00:20:55.756
Woodruff Library in Atlanta, and they,

00:20:55.756 --> 00:20:59.163
to give you an idea, she's
standing in an air handler.

00:20:59.163 --> 00:21:01.039
She's standing in one of those machines

00:21:01.039 --> 00:21:02.347
that make the environment.

00:21:02.347 --> 00:21:04.240
She's standing in front of a cooling coil

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:07.910
that's 12 feet high
and about 30 feet long.

00:21:07.910 --> 00:21:09.942
And there's another one of the same size

00:21:09.942 --> 00:21:12.025
directly behind this one.

00:21:14.022 --> 00:21:17.240
That machine, the cooling
that runs through that coil

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:19.696
is provided by two
machines, called chillers,

00:21:19.696 --> 00:21:21.836
that are of this size.

00:21:21.836 --> 00:21:25.456
So, when I say we're
talking about big money,

00:21:25.456 --> 00:21:27.191
you don't want to pay the electric bill.

00:21:27.191 --> 00:21:29.452
You don't want to pay
the fuel energy costs

00:21:29.452 --> 00:21:31.142
for these things.

00:21:31.142 --> 00:21:34.392
Okay, so here's where it comes down to.

00:21:35.742 --> 00:21:38.797
The scope of my interest and talk is

00:21:38.797 --> 00:21:42.741
in sustainability is not
building the greenest,

00:21:42.741 --> 00:21:46.824
coolest new thing, but
working with what we have.

00:21:47.713 --> 00:21:50.101
And that's going to be the
case for quite a while.

00:21:50.101 --> 00:21:52.609
The question for large institutions is,

00:21:52.609 --> 00:21:55.043
how do we work with the buildings we have,

00:21:55.043 --> 00:21:58.673
the mechanical systems, and
collections that we have?

00:21:58.673 --> 00:22:00.835
In other words, we want to optimize them.

00:22:00.835 --> 00:22:03.202
And to me this is where sustainability is

00:22:03.202 --> 00:22:04.869
defined in practice.

00:22:06.063 --> 00:22:08.230
What is optimal operation?

00:22:09.224 --> 00:22:12.666
Well, environment is the
best for the collection.

00:22:12.666 --> 00:22:15.270
It causes the least deterioration.

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:18.007
It uses the least energy, the least fuel,

00:22:18.007 --> 00:22:21.424
natural gas, electricity, and so on.

00:22:21.424 --> 00:22:23.830
That it, it's workable for the people

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:26.176
who have to use the collections.

00:22:26.176 --> 00:22:30.710
It's workable for the workflows
that the institution has

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:34.323
to actually fulfill its other functions.

00:22:34.323 --> 00:22:36.898
The best systems, most
sustainable systems,

00:22:36.898 --> 00:22:39.648
are also maintainable and simple.

00:22:40.731 --> 00:22:44.347
I cannot tell you how often
things were some designer's

00:22:44.347 --> 00:22:47.462
bright idea, and they're turned off.

00:22:47.462 --> 00:22:48.860
They're turned off.

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:50.638
People, I just,

00:22:50.638 --> 00:22:52.739
I arrived here to find out
that there have been layoffs

00:22:52.739 --> 00:22:55.567
in the facilities organization
in this institution.

00:22:55.567 --> 00:22:56.650
Recently.

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:58.673
And that's, no, they're not,

00:22:58.673 --> 00:23:00.930
you're not alone in that at all.

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:02.733
There's fewer and fewer people.

00:23:02.733 --> 00:23:04.470
They're stretched thinner and thinner.

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:07.162
The systems are more and more complicated.

00:23:07.162 --> 00:23:09.622
There is a huge premium on simplicity

00:23:09.622 --> 00:23:12.544
and maintainability in all this.

00:23:12.544 --> 00:23:15.692
And finally, in some
institutions, the structure itself

00:23:15.692 --> 00:23:17.418
is part of the collection.

00:23:17.418 --> 00:23:20.637
That's true for many
historic homes, and so on.

00:23:20.637 --> 00:23:22.492
Whatever the optimal solution is

00:23:22.492 --> 00:23:26.140
to operate these massive
energy-hog machines,

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:28.573
it has to work with the structure.

00:23:28.573 --> 00:23:31.212
It can't ruin the walls,
the building envelope.

00:23:31.212 --> 00:23:32.445
And it has to,

00:23:32.445 --> 00:23:37.031
it has to be working in harmony
with the outdoor climate.

00:23:37.031 --> 00:23:38.865
Okay, well that's the principles.

00:23:38.865 --> 00:23:42.150
Optimal, that's our
mantra and our buzzword.

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:44.425
Optimal operation.

00:23:44.425 --> 00:23:45.542
And that was what we set out

00:23:45.542 --> 00:23:47.464
to do at the Library of Congress,

00:23:47.464 --> 00:23:51.691
with the Mellon Foundation grant in 1999.

00:23:51.691 --> 00:23:53.926
And now we're in our eleventh year

00:23:53.926 --> 00:23:57.661
of working with the
preventive conservation staff

00:23:57.661 --> 00:23:59.879
and the Architect of the Capitol staff

00:23:59.879 --> 00:24:02.522
at the Library of Congress.

00:24:02.522 --> 00:24:04.237
And I'd like to just share with you

00:24:04.237 --> 00:24:06.415
a little bit of what we're doing and

00:24:06.415 --> 00:24:10.332
what that has meant, the
lessons we've learned.

00:24:11.659 --> 00:24:15.591
The way we've worked at
the Library of Congress is

00:24:15.591 --> 00:24:16.884
we work on two fronts.

00:24:16.884 --> 00:24:20.037
The energy front, and the collections

00:24:20.037 --> 00:24:22.287
environment front, at once.

00:24:23.476 --> 00:24:26.393
So we place, we place data loggers,

00:24:28.106 --> 00:24:30.522
this device here is called the PEM2,

00:24:30.522 --> 00:24:35.146
that's a temperature and
humidity recording device.

00:24:35.146 --> 00:24:39.515
It's about this big, and
it's battery-powered,

00:24:39.515 --> 00:24:43.294
very portable, and you can
retrieve the data from it

00:24:43.294 --> 00:24:47.913
by putting a USB flash drive
into a little port on the side,

00:24:47.913 --> 00:24:51.918
and it will write a text
file on that USB flash drive

00:24:51.918 --> 00:24:55.258
that tells you the temperature
and humidity conditions.

00:24:55.258 --> 00:25:00.246
And so they have approximately
150 such devices scattered

00:25:00.246 --> 00:25:04.463
in the three principle buildings
of the Library of Congress

00:25:04.463 --> 00:25:08.705
and they tell us what the
collections are experiencing.

00:25:08.705 --> 00:25:10.462
At the same time we're measuring

00:25:10.462 --> 00:25:13.807
inside selected air handling units,

00:25:13.807 --> 00:25:16.513
those machines that make the climate.

00:25:16.513 --> 00:25:20.690
And there you see June Wheeler,
by the way, was a partner

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:23.675
in an energy consulting firm
that we have worked with

00:25:23.675 --> 00:25:26.134
called Herzog, Wheeler and Associates.

00:25:26.134 --> 00:25:30.295
June, by the way, 1981 Yale
alumni in architecture.

00:25:30.295 --> 00:25:32.091
Yay, huh?
(audience laughs)

00:25:32.091 --> 00:25:34.750
Local girl makes good.
(audience laughs)

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:39.029
And that is Jeremy
Linden of the IPI staff.

00:25:39.029 --> 00:25:41.701
I'm sorry, messing it up here.

00:25:41.701 --> 00:25:46.186
And they are, they are
standing outside a large,

00:25:46.186 --> 00:25:49.299
but by no means the
largest ever, air handler

00:25:49.299 --> 00:25:53.433
that serves exactly half
of the Adams Building.

00:25:53.433 --> 00:25:58.210
The Adams Building is the
1934 building on Capitol Hill.

00:25:58.210 --> 00:26:01.612
It has 12 stack levels, I
don't know how many million

00:26:01.612 --> 00:26:05.779
volumes of books in one
giant block square building,

00:26:06.878 --> 00:26:09.462
and there are two air handlers that serve

00:26:09.462 --> 00:26:10.676
all those book stacks.

00:26:10.676 --> 00:26:13.509
The north unit and the south unit.

00:26:15.328 --> 00:26:18.066
So we are measuring the amount of energy

00:26:18.066 --> 00:26:22.066
that is consumed by the
south stack air handler.

00:26:23.819 --> 00:26:26.934
And what we've been doing is, recently is

00:26:26.934 --> 00:26:29.694
turning it off at eight o'clock at night

00:26:29.694 --> 00:26:32.296
and turning it on at six
o'clock in the morning.

00:26:32.296 --> 00:26:34.666
Shut off, dead, end of story.

00:26:34.666 --> 00:26:37.615
There are no staff in there at that time.

00:26:37.615 --> 00:26:38.698
And there is,

00:26:40.437 --> 00:26:43.687
there is no reason to have ventilation.

00:26:45.382 --> 00:26:49.049
Just by dint of this,
we have estimated that

00:26:50.889 --> 00:26:52.660
if we did that year-round.

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:55.616
If we turned it off every night,

00:26:55.616 --> 00:26:59.660
and turned it on again
every morning, year-round,

00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:02.884
it would save, in this unit alone,

00:27:02.884 --> 00:27:05.717
$80,000 US dollars in fuel energy.

00:27:07.622 --> 00:27:10.376
So between the two units,
if we did it in both sides,

00:27:10.376 --> 00:27:13.332
it would be $160,000 per year

00:27:13.332 --> 00:27:15.697
just by turning it off at night.

00:27:15.697 --> 00:27:18.792
So, and when I say it's
big money and big savings

00:27:18.792 --> 00:27:21.138
that are possible, it really is.

00:27:21.138 --> 00:27:23.367
But you have to do it safely.

00:27:23.367 --> 00:27:27.182
The trick is what happens
when you turn it off.

00:27:27.182 --> 00:27:30.954
And in the real world,
sometimes, very little happens.

00:27:30.954 --> 00:27:33.550
Happily, so far, that's been the case

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:36.394
in the Adams south stacks.

00:27:36.394 --> 00:27:39.126
You can see a tiny little blip,

00:27:39.126 --> 00:27:41.479
but we have the ability and the technology

00:27:41.479 --> 00:27:44.771
to know what that means
for the collection and

00:27:44.771 --> 00:27:47.118
it don't mean much.

00:27:47.118 --> 00:27:50.714
So, as long as the collection's
cruisin along happily,

00:27:50.714 --> 00:27:54.644
why not put that 80,000 bucks
in your pocket each year?

00:27:54.644 --> 00:27:57.528
And, just think, there
are two other buildings

00:27:57.528 --> 00:27:59.605
there at the Library of Congress.

00:27:59.605 --> 00:28:01.324
One of them, called the
James Madison Building,

00:28:01.324 --> 00:28:02.836
is the newest one.

00:28:02.836 --> 00:28:06.169
It has 42 air handlers in the penthouse,

00:28:07.134 --> 00:28:09.898
and another 16 air
handlers in the basement.

00:28:09.898 --> 00:28:11.035
You get the idea.

00:28:11.035 --> 00:28:14.301
I mean, I'm not trying
to oversell this, but

00:28:14.301 --> 00:28:16.168
there's real money involved,

00:28:16.168 --> 00:28:19.022
and it's really worth thinking about.

00:28:19.022 --> 00:28:22.886
Well, how do you address
that critical question of

00:28:22.886 --> 00:28:26.957
when is the collection,
as a result of anything,

00:28:26.957 --> 00:28:30.284
as a result of the phases
of the moons of Jupiter,

00:28:30.284 --> 00:28:33.532
or of you turning things off,
or just the way they did it,

00:28:33.532 --> 00:28:36.284
or just they way they've always done it.

00:28:36.284 --> 00:28:40.962
The problem is one of
prima facie understanding

00:28:40.962 --> 00:28:42.782
what the collections are experiencing,

00:28:42.782 --> 00:28:46.037
and what that might mean
in terms of deterioration.

00:28:46.037 --> 00:28:47.625
And this is a,

00:28:47.625 --> 00:28:48.580
this is a,

00:28:48.580 --> 00:28:52.165
give you an idea of how that
judgement call has been made

00:28:52.165 --> 00:28:56.166
not only here in Sterling,
but in many institutions,

00:28:56.166 --> 00:28:57.627
hundreds of different institutions

00:28:57.627 --> 00:29:01.907
are using our environmental
assessment methods,

00:29:01.907 --> 00:29:03.825
which begin by collecting temperature

00:29:03.825 --> 00:29:06.830
and humidity data continuousy.

00:29:06.830 --> 00:29:11.744
And, by the way, you ain't
got no data, you just punk.

00:29:11.744 --> 00:29:12.577
You know?

00:29:12.577 --> 00:29:14.345
What's that, what's that, uh,

00:29:14.345 --> 00:29:16.762
what's that saying, you know?

00:29:18.617 --> 00:29:22.099
Money talks, and something
else walks, you know?

00:29:22.099 --> 00:29:25.467
Data talks, and everything else walks.

00:29:25.467 --> 00:29:27.099
If you don't have data, if you don't know

00:29:27.099 --> 00:29:29.909
what your collections are experiencing,

00:29:29.909 --> 00:29:32.501
you're just kind of out of luck.

00:29:32.501 --> 00:29:34.824
Because you can't get to first base.

00:29:34.824 --> 00:29:37.601
I know there's a lot of
Red Sox fans here, right?

00:29:37.601 --> 00:29:39.415
You'd understand that analogy.

00:29:39.415 --> 00:29:41.047
Without temperature and humidity data,

00:29:41.047 --> 00:29:43.258
you don't get to first base.

00:29:43.258 --> 00:29:44.216
Alright.

00:29:44.216 --> 00:29:46.945
So, when you have temperature
and humidity data,

00:29:46.945 --> 00:29:49.596
a lot of people think that
they're able to then understand

00:29:49.596 --> 00:29:51.766
what the collections are experiencing.

00:29:51.766 --> 00:29:52.766
Yeah, right.

00:29:53.965 --> 00:29:55.495
That's what we spent 25 years of

00:29:55.495 --> 00:29:58.112
research to try to figure out is that

00:29:58.112 --> 00:30:01.813
surely there are ways to
take that basic information

00:30:01.813 --> 00:30:04.883
about temperature and moisture and then

00:30:04.883 --> 00:30:08.899
derive something that's
more meaningful to us that,

00:30:08.899 --> 00:30:12.721
that helps us understand
whether it's good or bad,

00:30:12.721 --> 00:30:15.632
or precisely how good or
bad it is for collections.

00:30:15.632 --> 00:30:19.589
So here's a kind of console
view that you might see.

00:30:19.589 --> 00:30:22.172
These are the different spaces.

00:30:23.231 --> 00:30:24.944
Here are forms of deterioration

00:30:24.944 --> 00:30:28.121
that can affect cultural heritage objects.

00:30:28.121 --> 00:30:30.348
Natural aging is slow chemical changes,

00:30:30.348 --> 00:30:32.879
that's a big one for libraries.

00:30:32.879 --> 00:30:36.487
Mechanical damage, that's due
to the sweling and shrinking.

00:30:36.487 --> 00:30:38.476
It's very important for
objects made of wood,

00:30:38.476 --> 00:30:40.893
or objects like paintings, or

00:30:43.403 --> 00:30:45.905
ivory or bone, or lacquered objects,

00:30:45.905 --> 00:30:49.999
things of that kind, where the
relative humidity of the air

00:30:49.999 --> 00:30:52.619
determines the moisture
content of the object,

00:30:52.619 --> 00:30:56.119
and through a dry winter or a damp summer,

00:30:57.254 --> 00:31:00.181
the object will expand and contract.

00:31:00.181 --> 00:31:03.021
How much it expands,
how much it contracts,

00:31:03.021 --> 00:31:07.136
and how big that excursion
is between its extremes

00:31:07.136 --> 00:31:09.178
of expansion and contraction.

00:31:09.178 --> 00:31:10.991
Those are parameters which you can use

00:31:10.991 --> 00:31:13.104
to manage the environment.

00:31:13.104 --> 00:31:15.854
And so, then there's mold growth.

00:31:16.735 --> 00:31:19.591
Mold will not grow under many

00:31:19.591 --> 00:31:21.039
common environmental conditions.

00:31:21.039 --> 00:31:24.081
We all know that damp
conditions promote mold growth.

00:31:24.081 --> 00:31:26.781
So, and then finally,
there's metal corrosion,

00:31:26.781 --> 00:31:29.623
which, again, requires a
certain amount of moisture.

00:31:29.623 --> 00:31:32.382
Well, the sort of console view to tell you

00:31:32.382 --> 00:31:35.891
what the collections are experiencing.

00:31:35.891 --> 00:31:37.637
One part of it can be boiled down

00:31:37.637 --> 00:31:40.041
to idiot lights, like this.

00:31:40.041 --> 00:31:42.364
Red, bad.
(audience laughs)

00:31:42.364 --> 00:31:44.606
Green, good.
(audience laughs)

00:31:44.606 --> 00:31:46.321
You know, and okay.

00:31:46.321 --> 00:31:47.762
Okay.
(audience laughs)

00:31:47.762 --> 00:31:49.163
So, you can see it.

00:31:49.163 --> 00:31:51.313
You know, you're just
like, "Okay, there it is."

00:31:51.313 --> 00:31:53.919
But you, you know, and
it's also dependent on

00:31:53.919 --> 00:31:56.810
what period of time
that you're looking at.

00:31:56.810 --> 00:31:59.778
If you want a little bit
more nuanced understanding of

00:31:59.778 --> 00:32:01.470
how good or bad the environment is

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:04.719
with respect to these
things that are critical

00:32:04.719 --> 00:32:08.314
to collections' care, then
you look at these numbers,

00:32:08.314 --> 00:32:10.959
which we call the preservation metrics.

00:32:10.959 --> 00:32:13.723
And these numbers are derived from the raw

00:32:13.723 --> 00:32:16.079
temperature and humidity data.

00:32:16.079 --> 00:32:20.139
And each one of them, I'm not
gonna explain them in detail,

00:32:20.139 --> 00:32:22.586
but that's the one that
relates to chemical aging.

00:32:22.586 --> 00:32:24.725
It's very important for fading of dyes

00:32:24.725 --> 00:32:27.398
and degradation of paper.

00:32:27.398 --> 00:32:28.724
That's a factor that tells you

00:32:28.724 --> 00:32:30.348
whether mold would be possible

00:32:30.348 --> 00:32:34.208
and when it's in red, it
says pay attention to me.

00:32:34.208 --> 00:32:37.171
These particular spaces
are all problematic

00:32:37.171 --> 00:32:39.058
from the natural aging point of view.

00:32:39.058 --> 00:32:41.418
One of them is beginning to be problematic

00:32:41.418 --> 00:32:43.541
from the mold point of view.

00:32:43.541 --> 00:32:45.473
And several of them are problematic

00:32:45.473 --> 00:32:47.270
because they get too damp.

00:32:47.270 --> 00:32:49.635
But I show you this only that,

00:32:49.635 --> 00:32:53.052
to explain that there are ways now to use

00:32:56.060 --> 00:32:58.764
quantitative quality control metrics

00:32:58.764 --> 00:33:01.135
to control preservation environments.

00:33:01.135 --> 00:33:03.485
And when they're in use, they work.

00:33:03.485 --> 00:33:07.754
And we've been using
those for a long time.

00:33:07.754 --> 00:33:09.659
Now, if you wanna,

00:33:09.659 --> 00:33:13.695
here's the really important
lesson that we've learned at LC.

00:33:13.695 --> 00:33:16.972
Environ, you know, the people who,

00:33:16.972 --> 00:33:18.556
Nobody raised their hand to say they were

00:33:18.556 --> 00:33:21.541
a building facilities type person, right?

00:33:21.541 --> 00:33:24.933
If you're there, you're
probably going like this now.

00:33:24.933 --> 00:33:26.104
(audience laughs)

00:33:26.104 --> 00:33:27.896
Let me say something sort of pejorative

00:33:27.896 --> 00:33:31.049
and stereotypical about that role.

00:33:31.049 --> 00:33:34.352
It's not really
pejorative, it's real life.

00:33:34.352 --> 00:33:37.740
If you or I were in that role,
we'd be doing the same thing

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:41.240
which is that, when you
think about environment,

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:45.209
they think about environment as a project.

00:33:45.209 --> 00:33:47.402
Environment is, yeah, well we'll

00:33:47.402 --> 00:33:49.526
get the equipment to do the job.

00:33:49.526 --> 00:33:50.975
They don't think about it the way we,

00:33:50.975 --> 00:33:55.117
I think you should approach
it, which is as a process.

00:33:55.117 --> 00:33:59.656
Really, facilities people,
it's either a project

00:33:59.656 --> 00:34:03.260
that has to be designed and
built and scoped and budgeted,

00:34:03.260 --> 00:34:05.177
or it's a trouble call,

00:34:06.616 --> 00:34:08.703
or a comfort complaint.

00:34:08.703 --> 00:34:09.666
That's all there is.

00:34:09.666 --> 00:34:13.167
There's no, like, look at it every day,

00:34:13.167 --> 00:34:14.089
see how it's doing.

00:34:14.089 --> 00:34:16.104
Or look at it every month, you know?

00:34:16.104 --> 00:34:18.658
It's a project, or it's a trouble call,

00:34:18.658 --> 00:34:20.652
or it's a human comfort complaint.

00:34:20.652 --> 00:34:22.524
And humans can use the telephone

00:34:22.524 --> 00:34:24.164
and they can call up
and say, "I'm too hot",

00:34:24.164 --> 00:34:28.318
"I'm too cold", and that
will drive the actions.

00:34:28.318 --> 00:34:31.735
That's one key feedback mechanism for the

00:34:32.854 --> 00:34:34.287
building operators.

00:34:34.287 --> 00:34:36.002
But the collections,

00:34:36.002 --> 00:34:37.399
they lost the number.

00:34:37.399 --> 00:34:39.059
I mean it was so heart-breaking.

00:34:39.059 --> 00:34:39.892
(audience laughs)

00:34:39.892 --> 00:34:42.248
They lost that building guy's number.

00:34:42.248 --> 00:34:44.253
You know, they had it written
down on a Post-it note,

00:34:44.253 --> 00:34:46.019
and they stuck it somewhere.

00:34:46.019 --> 00:34:48.054
And now it fell off,
and now they, you know,

00:34:48.054 --> 00:34:49.985
the books can't call anybody anymore.

00:34:49.985 --> 00:34:52.318
So that's, that's, you know,

00:34:53.825 --> 00:34:57.713
if you're going to address
and change that circumstance,

00:34:57.713 --> 00:35:00.523
it has to, you have to go after it.

00:35:00.523 --> 00:35:01.749
It's a process.

00:35:01.749 --> 00:35:04.275
It's people getting together and talk.

00:35:04.275 --> 00:35:06.801
It's not a numerical
specification, by the way.

00:35:06.801 --> 00:35:08.773
Managing an environment is not

00:35:08.773 --> 00:35:11.468
is not calling up your buddy
at the other conservation lab

00:35:11.468 --> 00:35:13.001
and saying, "well, what do you do?"

00:35:13.001 --> 00:35:14.610
"Well, we do 70, 50."

00:35:14.610 --> 00:35:18.012
"Okay, that's what I'm gonna
tell the architect we want."

00:35:18.012 --> 00:35:19.646
That isn't managing your environment.

00:35:19.646 --> 00:35:21.866
It's not a numerical specification.

00:35:21.866 --> 00:35:24.098
In fact, no single environment

00:35:24.098 --> 00:35:26.378
is best for every kind of object.

00:35:26.378 --> 00:35:30.047
So it can't be approached that way.

00:35:30.047 --> 00:35:32.826
And really, the way to think
about environmental management

00:35:32.826 --> 00:35:35.975
for preservation and sustainability is

00:35:35.975 --> 00:35:38.689
by analogy to modern manufacturing.

00:35:38.689 --> 00:35:43.675
Think about the way that
automobiles are manufactured.

00:35:43.675 --> 00:35:46.650
And, famously, even though Toyota's

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:49.561
having a lot of problems lately (laughs),

00:35:49.561 --> 00:35:50.489
people in the, do you know,

00:35:50.489 --> 00:35:51.499
did you read that in the paper?

00:35:51.499 --> 00:35:53.771
People still buy Toyotas, okay?

00:35:53.771 --> 00:35:55.153
They're not, they're not hurtin.

00:35:55.153 --> 00:35:58.243
Akio Toyota is not about to commit seppuku

00:35:58.243 --> 00:36:01.550
because his gas pedals stuck a little bit.

00:36:01.550 --> 00:36:03.852
They still sell them and
people still buy them, why?

00:36:03.852 --> 00:36:05.795
Because they're really well made.

00:36:05.795 --> 00:36:09.962
And the manufacturing prowess
of Japan is well known.

00:36:10.999 --> 00:36:14.280
And they invented these concepts

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:16.833
of continuous improvement in many cases.

00:36:16.833 --> 00:36:19.079
And if you're in modern manufacturing,

00:36:19.079 --> 00:36:22.080
modern manufacturing
is done on these ideas

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:23.588
that it's never finished.

00:36:23.588 --> 00:36:26.012
It's a process of continuous examination

00:36:26.012 --> 00:36:27.959
of the production line, of the costs

00:36:27.959 --> 00:36:29.848
that goes into producing the product.

00:36:29.848 --> 00:36:33.473
It's a constant reassessment
of quality control.

00:36:33.473 --> 00:36:35.226
And if we could make that analogy,

00:36:35.226 --> 00:36:38.586
the environment is something
that's manufactured every day,

00:36:38.586 --> 00:36:40.954
down there, those machines, those AHUs,

00:36:40.954 --> 00:36:42.709
they're not stamping out widgets,

00:36:42.709 --> 00:36:45.665
they're stamping out temperature
and humidity conditions.

00:36:45.665 --> 00:36:48.498
And if we don't pay attention
to how they're working,

00:36:48.498 --> 00:36:50.685
we're not gonna ever get any better.

00:36:50.685 --> 00:36:54.005
So, what's preservation's role in this

00:36:54.005 --> 00:36:57.833
modern manufacturing process,
if we can make that analogy?

00:36:57.833 --> 00:37:00.734
The preservation role is as designer,

00:37:00.734 --> 00:37:03.519
because, after all,
it's not the facilities,

00:37:03.519 --> 00:37:05.553
it's not the library directors,

00:37:05.553 --> 00:37:08.703
it's the preservation
and conservation role

00:37:08.703 --> 00:37:11.100
to say what the environment should be.

00:37:11.100 --> 00:37:13.167
To lay out its key points.

00:37:13.167 --> 00:37:16.481
And then it's up to other
people to manufacture it.

00:37:16.481 --> 00:37:17.903
But then they have another key role,

00:37:17.903 --> 00:37:19.238
which is quality control,

00:37:19.238 --> 00:37:22.918
which is what I just showed
you with using the metrics.

00:37:22.918 --> 00:37:23.751
And you know what?

00:37:23.751 --> 00:37:27.259
Metrics are found in every
area of manufacturing.

00:37:27.259 --> 00:37:29.824
And you can't improve
automobile production lines

00:37:29.824 --> 00:37:32.671
unless you have some
way of measuring defects

00:37:32.671 --> 00:37:34.146
and reducing them.

00:37:34.146 --> 00:37:37.753
And, so, you're part of a production team

00:37:37.753 --> 00:37:39.612
if you're in the preservation world.

00:37:39.612 --> 00:37:42.986
And that production team
is making the environment.

00:37:42.986 --> 00:37:46.149
Whose goal is optimal operation.

00:37:46.149 --> 00:37:49.013
Remember all those things
working together in harmony?

00:37:49.013 --> 00:37:50.840
You know, woo.

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:52.885
It just gives me goosebumps
to think about it.

00:37:52.885 --> 00:37:55.245
(audience laughs)

00:37:55.245 --> 00:37:58.142
But the participants in this process are

00:37:58.142 --> 00:38:00.117
the collection care professionals,

00:38:00.117 --> 00:38:02.099
the building operators themselves,

00:38:02.099 --> 00:38:05.248
the people who love
and own the collection,

00:38:05.248 --> 00:38:08.956
the curators, librarians,
archivists, and the staff,

00:38:08.956 --> 00:38:11.869
and then the administrators
who oversee the whole thing,

00:38:11.869 --> 00:38:14.266
including the money people.

00:38:14.266 --> 00:38:18.080
And they all together share
the responsibilities for

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:20.187
stewardship of the collections,

00:38:20.187 --> 00:38:23.685
for cost control, because
people are getting laid off

00:38:23.685 --> 00:38:26.678
because these systems are
chewing up too much money,

00:38:26.678 --> 00:38:30.002
and for sustainability in the sense of

00:38:30.002 --> 00:38:32.669
being a responsible global citizen.

00:38:32.669 --> 00:38:35.836
I mean, it should bother everyone that

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:39.370
unnecessary, if there's
unnecessary energy waste,

00:38:39.370 --> 00:38:40.857
it is harming the planet.

00:38:40.857 --> 00:38:42.960
And it is spending money that

00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:45.901
could be used for much better things.

00:38:45.901 --> 00:38:47.401
I had to throw this in!

00:38:47.401 --> 00:38:50.110
You know, I, I'm a former English major,

00:38:50.110 --> 00:38:52.742
and I love that analogy of manufacturing,

00:38:52.742 --> 00:38:54.843
you know, and continuous improvement.

00:38:54.843 --> 00:38:58.318
And today I got this email
and I and to throw it in here.

00:38:58.318 --> 00:39:00.901
At RIT, where I work, you know,

00:39:02.282 --> 00:39:05.843
there was an email advertising
RIT's Lean Six Sigma

00:39:05.843 --> 00:39:09.150
Yellow Belt Certification Program.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:11.189
Now, have you all had that
conversation with somebody

00:39:11.189 --> 00:39:12.704
and you're saying, well what do you do?

00:39:12.704 --> 00:39:16.576
And they say "I'm a six sigma black belt."

00:39:16.576 --> 00:39:17.457
(audience laughs)

00:39:17.457 --> 00:39:18.680
You never had that conversation?

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:21.254
(laughs)
(audience laughs)

00:39:21.254 --> 00:39:24.588
Well, let me break this down for you.

00:39:24.588 --> 00:39:26.457
Okay, well, you know what lean is.

00:39:26.457 --> 00:39:28.678
You know, lean is like in manufac,

00:39:28.678 --> 00:39:30.749
this is manufacturing jargon.

00:39:30.749 --> 00:39:32.761
And this is where manufacturing is.

00:39:32.761 --> 00:39:33.878
If you want, you know,

00:39:33.878 --> 00:39:36.511
we're a school that teaches
real place manufacturing,

00:39:36.511 --> 00:39:39.012
as much as we've got left in this country.

00:39:39.012 --> 00:39:41.455
You know, and lean
manufacturing means you're,

00:39:41.455 --> 00:39:43.556
you don't have any unnecessary parts,

00:39:43.556 --> 00:39:46.037
inventory, and all of that.

00:39:46.037 --> 00:39:49.310
Six sigma, everybody
in tune with six sigma?

00:39:49.310 --> 00:39:51.656
How many sigmas you got here?

00:39:51.656 --> 00:39:53.551
(laughs)

00:39:53.551 --> 00:39:54.674
Do you, do you have, seriously,

00:39:54.674 --> 00:39:57.253
everybody not know what six sigma is?

00:39:57.253 --> 00:39:59.656
How many people not
know what six sigma is?

00:39:59.656 --> 00:40:00.686
(audience murmurs)

00:40:00.686 --> 00:40:03.097
Oh, god, you people are
hopelessly out of touch.

00:40:03.097 --> 00:40:04.319
(audience laughs)

00:40:04.319 --> 00:40:05.152
- [Woman] Yeah.

00:40:05.152 --> 00:40:06.048
- [James] Yeah.

00:40:06.048 --> 00:40:08.190
When you do real quality
control and you have those

00:40:08.190 --> 00:40:12.220
measurements of quality
like you're making widgets,

00:40:12.220 --> 00:40:13.553
six sigma means,

00:40:14.468 --> 00:40:16.288
sigma is the, is the,

00:40:16.288 --> 00:40:20.455
is the measure of the number
of wrongly-manufactured parts,

00:40:21.761 --> 00:40:24.051
for the sake of thinking about it.

00:40:24.051 --> 00:40:25.499
And six sigma is

00:40:25.499 --> 00:40:26.582
0.00001%

00:40:31.288 --> 00:40:35.283
of parts are made in a defective manner.

00:40:35.283 --> 00:40:36.619
So if you're six sigma,

00:40:36.619 --> 00:40:39.154
like if you work for
Motorola making cellphones,

00:40:39.154 --> 00:40:41.974
Motorola is obsessed with six sigma.

00:40:41.974 --> 00:40:46.026
There are people whose,
whose careers are six sigma.

00:40:46.026 --> 00:40:48.177
And so many so that
they had to have, like,

00:40:48.177 --> 00:40:52.481
six sigma newbie, you know,
white, white, you know,

00:40:52.481 --> 00:40:54.708
and it's like the dojo where
you're learning karate.

00:40:54.708 --> 00:40:55.880
(audience laughs)

00:40:55.880 --> 00:40:58.742
First, you get the robe and
you feel all self-conscious.

00:40:58.742 --> 00:41:01.788
And, you know, and then you
get, what's the first belt

00:41:01.788 --> 00:41:03.847
you get white belt or something?

00:41:03.847 --> 00:41:06.587
And then you progress up
through brown belt, yellow belt,

00:41:06.587 --> 00:41:09.689
and if you're black
belt, and then you know.

00:41:09.689 --> 00:41:12.376
So, like this, this
program here that they're

00:41:12.376 --> 00:41:15.589
advertising in emails
in my campus, is like,

00:41:15.589 --> 00:41:17.405
"Yeah, you can sign up
for this three-day course

00:41:17.405 --> 00:41:19.973
"to learn how to do quality control

00:41:19.973 --> 00:41:23.556
"so that you are at
0.00001 defects", okay?

00:41:27.976 --> 00:41:31.393
So that, manufacturing today does embody,

00:41:32.677 --> 00:41:34.626
if you read through
the text at the bottom,

00:41:34.626 --> 00:41:38.735
it does embody complicated
things done cooperatively.

00:41:38.735 --> 00:41:41.568
And my thesis here in this talk is that

00:41:41.568 --> 00:41:44.618
that's the way I see
institutional preservation

00:41:44.618 --> 00:41:47.798
and sustainability
programs working together.

00:41:47.798 --> 00:41:49.608
To understand that it's a process.

00:41:49.608 --> 00:41:51.818
To understand people's responsibilities

00:41:51.818 --> 00:41:54.397
and people hold up their end of the deal.

00:41:54.397 --> 00:41:56.627
It's absolutely hopeless, by the way,

00:41:56.627 --> 00:41:58.286
to expect that facilities people

00:41:58.286 --> 00:42:02.733
would understand collections
care and preservation.

00:42:02.733 --> 00:42:05.609
But they need to know enough about it

00:42:05.609 --> 00:42:08.299
that they can function
as part of the team.

00:42:08.299 --> 00:42:10.049
And therefore, we as,

00:42:11.096 --> 00:42:13.658
Oh, well, this is Chip and Kevin.

00:42:13.658 --> 00:42:16.598
This is part of the Library
of Congress project, too.

00:42:16.598 --> 00:42:20.763
I mean when we first
started there in 1999,

00:42:20.763 --> 00:42:23.091
we did not have wondrous cooperation

00:42:23.091 --> 00:42:26.501
from the building
operators, but now we do.

00:42:26.501 --> 00:42:29.613
And it's really proof that, I mean,

00:42:29.613 --> 00:42:31.838
now when we work there,

00:42:31.838 --> 00:42:34.329
and these guys take care
of the Adams building,

00:42:34.329 --> 00:42:36.598
and they are the front
lines of the sustainability

00:42:36.598 --> 00:42:39.192
in the Library of Congress
in this respect that

00:42:39.192 --> 00:42:41.789
if anybody's gonna save the $80,000,

00:42:41.789 --> 00:42:43.826
it's gonna be Chip and Kevin.

00:42:43.826 --> 00:42:46.408
And they have to understand
what's going on, and

00:42:46.408 --> 00:42:48.538
and they do, and it's a real
pleasure to work with them.

00:42:48.538 --> 00:42:51.212
So, I know the process can work.

00:42:51.212 --> 00:42:53.880
And the man on the right
in the black is, there,

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:56.133
that's Peter Herzog.

00:42:56.133 --> 00:42:58.371
He's the energy specialist on our team.

00:42:58.371 --> 00:43:02.235
And the person on the left
is, works at IPI, Jeremy.

00:43:02.235 --> 00:43:04.368
He'll be working here in Sterling.

00:43:04.368 --> 00:43:07.423
They are standing in that
air handler that serves

00:43:07.423 --> 00:43:10.774
Adams south, and that gives you some idea

00:43:10.774 --> 00:43:14.041
that that is a large
machine, and indeed it is.

00:43:14.041 --> 00:43:15.252
And that is Ben Bauman.

00:43:15.252 --> 00:43:19.085
He's the preservation
liaison for the project.

00:43:19.925 --> 00:43:23.024
And he's pointing to
one of our data loggers

00:43:23.024 --> 00:43:24.627
that is helping us to understand

00:43:24.627 --> 00:43:27.786
when the system goes on and goes off.

00:43:27.786 --> 00:43:32.395
So, I wanted to emphasize
that the preservation role

00:43:32.395 --> 00:43:35.243
in this, you don't have
to know what these,

00:43:35.243 --> 00:43:36.408
you don't have to know the building

00:43:36.408 --> 00:43:38.027
so well you could fix everything.

00:43:38.027 --> 00:43:42.361
You don't have to know the
energy saving part so well

00:43:42.361 --> 00:43:45.670
that you can puzzle out the BTUs.

00:43:45.670 --> 00:43:48.676
But you have to know
enough to be a team player

00:43:48.676 --> 00:43:51.772
And you do have to take possession

00:43:51.772 --> 00:43:55.272
of gathering data and using these metrics.

00:43:56.229 --> 00:43:58.632
And you do have to know
enough about your collection

00:43:58.632 --> 00:44:01.654
to know what its particular needs are.

00:44:01.654 --> 00:44:06.354
And you can't just read
it in a book, and so on.

00:44:06.354 --> 00:44:10.358
That's the data logger
I was talking about.

00:44:10.358 --> 00:44:14.478
Now I'm gonna beat up for
a while on the need to

00:44:14.478 --> 00:44:17.811
to have a close idea of quality control.

00:44:19.500 --> 00:44:21.862
Most people who collect
temperature and humidity data

00:44:21.862 --> 00:44:23.904
just see it graphed like this,

00:44:23.904 --> 00:44:27.212
which is impossible to
understand and analyze.

00:44:27.212 --> 00:44:29.572
Yes, if you've been doing it for 15 years,

00:44:29.572 --> 00:44:31.445
you get some idea.

00:44:31.445 --> 00:44:34.709
But it's a far cry from
what I just showed you.

00:44:34.709 --> 00:44:37.736
And a lot of people's idea
about the environment,

00:44:37.736 --> 00:44:40.119
and this comes from the museum world,

00:44:40.119 --> 00:44:43.331
is that the ideal environment
for collections is

00:44:43.331 --> 00:44:47.712
very steady, it's about
70 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:44:47.712 --> 00:44:51.865
comfortable for humans, and
then doesn't vary very much.

00:44:51.865 --> 00:44:55.532
And it's 50% RH, kind of the
middle of the humidity range,

00:44:55.532 --> 00:44:59.083
and it doesn't vary more
than, say, 5% up or down.

00:44:59.083 --> 00:45:02.574
That, you might say, is the
current museum standard.

00:45:02.574 --> 00:45:05.362
Next Monday and Tuesday is a meeting,

00:45:05.362 --> 00:45:08.749
a very high-level summit meeting,

00:45:08.749 --> 00:45:12.215
at the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston

00:45:12.215 --> 00:45:14.246
by museum conservators and others.

00:45:14.246 --> 00:45:17.073
I will, I'll be attending,

00:45:17.073 --> 00:45:20.752
I'll probably have to wear a helmet, but

00:45:20.752 --> 00:45:23.068
I will be attending (laughs).

00:45:23.068 --> 00:45:25.177
The whole idea is that even for the

00:45:25.177 --> 00:45:27.344
real high end art museums,

00:45:28.250 --> 00:45:31.083
this hand-me-down standard is not,

00:45:32.020 --> 00:45:34.771
is under attack, it's not workable.

00:45:34.771 --> 00:45:36.462
It is good for one thing.

00:45:36.462 --> 00:45:40.263
70 degrees, 50% RH is
not the ideal environment

00:45:40.263 --> 00:45:41.637
for your collection.

00:45:41.637 --> 00:45:45.554
It has, however, one
absolutely useful feature.

00:45:47.985 --> 00:45:49.695
If you're gonna be exhibiting stuff,

00:45:49.695 --> 00:45:52.593
it's convenient for the visitors
who come to the exhibit.

00:45:52.593 --> 00:45:53.511
And if you're gonna be putting

00:45:53.511 --> 00:45:55.582
different types of objects together,

00:45:55.582 --> 00:45:59.490
the 50% RH level is
probably the best compromise

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:02.245
for showing different kinds of objects.

00:46:02.245 --> 00:46:05.777
But that's as far as its
beauty and wonderment go.

00:46:05.777 --> 00:46:08.237
Because, as the next dot says,

00:46:08.237 --> 00:46:10.536
it's mad money for most climates.

00:46:10.536 --> 00:46:12.382
It really is very expensive.

00:46:12.382 --> 00:46:14.929
It's kind of the enemy of sustainability.

00:46:14.929 --> 00:46:16.691
Because if that's the way,

00:46:16.691 --> 00:46:18.500
if that's your position,

00:46:18.500 --> 00:46:22.534
you want it steadily
70 degrees and 50% RH,

00:46:22.534 --> 00:46:25.995
you're kind of locked out
of the sustainability game.

00:46:25.995 --> 00:46:27.601
And you're paying mad money for that.

00:46:27.601 --> 00:46:30.640
Mad money in the street sense
of that's a lot of money.

00:46:30.640 --> 00:46:33.145
And mad money in the
other sense of mad, like,

00:46:33.145 --> 00:46:34.817
you're mad if you do that.

00:46:34.817 --> 00:46:36.984
Because of, you know, bah!

00:46:37.910 --> 00:46:39.001
And it's in, like I say,

00:46:39.001 --> 00:46:42.315
it's incompatible with
sustainable operations.

00:46:42.315 --> 00:46:44.186
And here in a library,

00:46:44.186 --> 00:46:48.409
70, 50 is really bad
for library collections.

00:46:48.409 --> 00:46:52.458
Because it's too warm,
you're cooking the books.

00:46:52.458 --> 00:46:54.481
I talked about the metrics.

00:46:54.481 --> 00:46:56.467
They really allow you to do that.

00:46:56.467 --> 00:46:58.041
And I wanted to point out another website

00:46:58.041 --> 00:47:01.541
that's very instructive called dpcalc.org.

00:47:02.695 --> 00:47:05.493
And this is a website that we created

00:47:05.493 --> 00:47:07.938
where you can explore
the inner relationship

00:47:07.938 --> 00:47:10.724
between temperature,
humidity, and dew point.

00:47:10.724 --> 00:47:13.449
And you can see on the right-hand side,

00:47:13.449 --> 00:47:16.745
those idiot lights and the
values of those metrics,

00:47:16.745 --> 00:47:20.912
and you can see how there is
no one perfect environment.

00:47:21.928 --> 00:47:23.337
There's a continuum.

00:47:23.337 --> 00:47:25.756
And some environments are better for

00:47:25.756 --> 00:47:28.615
preserving objects that
might degrade through

00:47:28.615 --> 00:47:31.598
spontaneous chemical change,
like paper and textiles.

00:47:31.598 --> 00:47:34.795
Some environments are better for,

00:47:34.795 --> 00:47:38.365
for other kinds of materials,
or assemblages of materials.

00:47:38.365 --> 00:47:40.378
And you can explore that here.

00:47:40.378 --> 00:47:42.966
It's a very good teaching tool.

00:47:42.966 --> 00:47:43.989
Tara was saying to me.

00:47:43.989 --> 00:47:46.749
Tara Kennedy, the preservation
field services officer

00:47:46.749 --> 00:47:49.493
here at Sterling, was
saying to me just today,

00:47:49.493 --> 00:47:51.865
this is, if you don't know this,

00:47:51.865 --> 00:47:53.570
you don't know nothing (laughs).

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:54.471
(audience laughs)

00:47:54.471 --> 00:47:57.055
Because this is the
fundamental relationships

00:47:57.055 --> 00:47:58.732
that guide the whole thing,

00:47:58.732 --> 00:48:00.488
whether you're trying
to make the environment

00:48:00.488 --> 00:48:02.811
or you're trying to understand it.

00:48:02.811 --> 00:48:05.598
Anyway, the bottom line,
in our 11 years at LC,

00:48:05.598 --> 00:48:07.393
and there you see Nancy Lev-Alexander,

00:48:07.393 --> 00:48:08.726
who is the, the,

00:48:12.036 --> 00:48:15.251
preventive conservation
supervisor at the library,

00:48:15.251 --> 00:48:18.936
together with Peter Herzog
and Ben, who works for Nancy.

00:48:18.936 --> 00:48:23.017
And they, the lesson
that we've learned is,

00:48:23.017 --> 00:48:25.782
remember going back to
that optimal operation?

00:48:25.782 --> 00:48:28.264
If you think that your place might be,

00:48:28.264 --> 00:48:30.998
by chance, optimal already?

00:48:30.998 --> 00:48:32.948
(laughs) Forget about it.

00:48:32.948 --> 00:48:35.029
(audience laughs)
Just forget about it.

00:48:35.029 --> 00:48:37.158
That's one thing we've
seen over and over again

00:48:37.158 --> 00:48:39.804
is that you can safely
assume it's not optimal

00:48:39.804 --> 00:48:43.682
unless you've really looked at
it and tried to make it that.

00:48:43.682 --> 00:48:45.542
The last thing I wanted to share with you

00:48:45.542 --> 00:48:46.792
was a new idea,

00:48:48.465 --> 00:48:52.578
that is particular, really,
I think to museum objects,

00:48:52.578 --> 00:48:54.502
and particular to certain climates,

00:48:54.502 --> 00:48:59.389
but it's very interesting from
the low-energy point of view.

00:48:59.389 --> 00:49:03.497
The Danes, when the Arab Oil
embargo hit in the 1970s,

00:49:03.497 --> 00:49:04.803
there were,

00:49:04.803 --> 00:49:07.173
the Danes had a reaction like,

00:49:07.173 --> 00:49:10.918
oh, we gotta save energy from now on.

00:49:10.918 --> 00:49:12.250
And they've been at it ever since

00:49:12.250 --> 00:49:14.254
in a very very serious way.

00:49:14.254 --> 00:49:15.973
Our reaction was,

00:49:15.973 --> 00:49:18.786
ah, gee, there's a long line
at the gas station (laughs).

00:49:18.786 --> 00:49:19.619
(audience laughs)

00:49:19.619 --> 00:49:21.094
I can't wait 'til that's over.

00:49:21.094 --> 00:49:21.927
Oh, it's over!

00:49:21.927 --> 00:49:23.222
Okay!

00:49:23.222 --> 00:49:26.759
And we went on, happily
ignoring the whole darn thing.

00:49:26.759 --> 00:49:29.859
And, you know, it's interesting
to see what you could do

00:49:29.859 --> 00:49:31.865
if you didn't do that.

00:49:31.865 --> 00:49:35.094
And at the National Museum of Denmark,

00:49:35.094 --> 00:49:38.216
they have a group of
scientists and engineers

00:49:38.216 --> 00:49:41.000
that work exclusively with the idea

00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:44.119
of designing buildings from the ground up

00:49:44.119 --> 00:49:48.119
that are storage for
archives and museum objects

00:49:49.146 --> 00:49:51.640
and they used very very little energy.

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:53.610
And I wanted to show you two of them.

00:49:53.610 --> 00:49:57.333
One of them is in the
town of Vejle in Denmark.

00:49:57.333 --> 00:49:58.596
It is a museum storage building

00:49:58.596 --> 00:50:02.112
that is a cooperative
of among seven museums.

00:50:02.112 --> 00:50:05.819
And then its son or daughter,
or whatever you wanna call it,

00:50:05.819 --> 00:50:08.630
another building for another
museum in another town

00:50:08.630 --> 00:50:11.693
that was designed along
the same principles.

00:50:11.693 --> 00:50:13.810
So, hey, you know you're not in Kansas

00:50:13.810 --> 00:50:16.853
when you see a building like this, right?

00:50:16.853 --> 00:50:19.859
This is the Vejle Cooperative Storehouse.

00:50:19.859 --> 00:50:21.902
(clears throat) Doesn't look like much.

00:50:21.902 --> 00:50:24.184
It's not winning any
awards for landscaping

00:50:24.184 --> 00:50:26.000
I don't think, either.

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:29.751
Um, it's not exactly the
architectural triumph of the,

00:50:29.751 --> 00:50:33.918
of Europe, but that's what
it looks like on the outside.

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:37.030
(clears throat) The really
cool part, and this is

00:50:37.030 --> 00:50:39.246
what it looks like on the inside.

00:50:39.246 --> 00:50:41.413
It's 600 square meters of,

00:50:42.326 --> 00:50:44.973
about 30 feet high ceilings.

00:50:44.973 --> 00:50:46.477
It's a one-story building.

00:50:46.477 --> 00:50:49.285
It needs to be to be the kind of

00:50:49.285 --> 00:50:51.053
very low-energy building that it is.

00:50:51.053 --> 00:50:52.590
I'll explain that in a second.

00:50:52.590 --> 00:50:53.901
But that gives you a feeling for the

00:50:53.901 --> 00:50:56.484
kind of objects that are there.

00:50:57.486 --> 00:51:01.049
The Vejle environment is completely,

00:51:01.049 --> 00:51:05.037
the environment inside
it is a prize-winner.

00:51:05.037 --> 00:51:07.526
On our metrics, and by any estimation,

00:51:07.526 --> 00:51:08.609
it is a real,

00:51:10.033 --> 00:51:11.116
it's a champ.

00:51:12.330 --> 00:51:16.856
The RH is steady at
about 55% all year long.

00:51:16.856 --> 00:51:18.521
The temperature varies.

00:51:18.521 --> 00:51:20.450
This is a basic design.

00:51:20.450 --> 00:51:23.565
The RH is steady, and
the temperature varies.

00:51:23.565 --> 00:51:26.603
And the temperature goes
from about 45 degrees

00:51:26.603 --> 00:51:30.842
inside in winter, to about
65 degrees in summer.

00:51:30.842 --> 00:51:35.635
And there are never any violent
spikes in relative humidity

00:51:35.635 --> 00:51:38.762
because the interior
walls, which are made of a

00:51:38.762 --> 00:51:41.845
special concrete, and the
collections themseves,

00:51:41.845 --> 00:51:45.468
to some degree buffer
the relative humidity.

00:51:45.468 --> 00:51:48.528
Here's some of the secrets
to making this building

00:51:48.528 --> 00:51:50.414
do its magic.

00:51:50.414 --> 00:51:52.247
It has no outside air.

00:51:53.154 --> 00:51:55.933
There's no intentionally-introduced
outside air.

00:51:55.933 --> 00:51:58.893
From leakage, to doors opening, and so on,

00:51:58.893 --> 00:52:00.317
there have a measured it to be

00:52:00.317 --> 00:52:03.704
about 1-1/2 air changes per day.

00:52:03.704 --> 00:52:06.608
So, the inside air is
replaced by outside air

00:52:06.608 --> 00:52:08.687
only 1-1/2 times a day.

00:52:08.687 --> 00:52:10.523
Does that sound like a lot to you?

00:52:10.523 --> 00:52:12.088
A building like this?

00:52:12.088 --> 00:52:16.088
Probably undergoes 17 to
20 air changes per day.

00:52:17.036 --> 00:52:18.416
The room we're in.

00:52:18.416 --> 00:52:20.890
So, that's very low.

00:52:20.890 --> 00:52:22.762
And as far as quality goes,

00:52:22.762 --> 00:52:24.510
the TWPI is over 100.

00:52:24.510 --> 00:52:29.445
Here in Sterling, most of
the TWPIs are about 50, 55.

00:52:29.445 --> 00:52:30.800
There's very little dimensional change

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:33.416
because the RH hardly varies
at all throughout the year.

00:52:33.416 --> 00:52:36.456
There's no mold because
55% RH won't stand,

00:52:36.456 --> 00:52:38.396
won't support mold.

00:52:38.396 --> 00:52:40.734
And here's the punchline.

00:52:40.734 --> 00:52:42.700
Here's the money shot.

00:52:42.700 --> 00:52:43.745
Total energy cost?

00:52:43.745 --> 00:52:45.495
$3 per kilowatt-hour,

00:52:46.742 --> 00:52:47.659
$3 per kil,

00:52:49.136 --> 00:52:51.863
Okay, I'm screwing up my money line.

00:52:51.863 --> 00:52:52.810
(audience laughs)

00:52:52.810 --> 00:52:54.892
To operate the building,

00:52:54.892 --> 00:52:55.809
it costs $3

00:52:56.668 --> 00:52:58.144
per cubic meter,

00:52:58.144 --> 00:52:59.385
per year.

00:52:59.385 --> 00:53:00.468
And that, um,

00:53:01.974 --> 00:53:04.635
I think, yeah, I mis-labeled the slide.

00:53:04.635 --> 00:53:06.492
But it's $3 per cubic meter.

00:53:06.492 --> 00:53:09.310
Now, it's a floor area
of 600 square meters,

00:53:09.310 --> 00:53:12.951
and it's 30 feet high,
so, you get the idea.

00:53:12.951 --> 00:53:14.578
But that is peanuts.

00:53:14.578 --> 00:53:15.745
It is peanuts.

00:53:16.683 --> 00:53:18.135
And another concern when.

00:53:18.135 --> 00:53:19.999
Okay, if you all your museum objects

00:53:19.999 --> 00:53:22.910
in a closed box like this,

00:53:22.910 --> 00:53:24.327
how come, it, it.

00:53:25.292 --> 00:53:26.810
Doesn't foul its own nest?

00:53:26.810 --> 00:53:30.750
Doesn't the collection
objects produce pollutants?

00:53:30.750 --> 00:53:34.565
And by careful selection,
you'll notice it has,

00:53:34.565 --> 00:53:37.836
it's basically all metal,
except for the collections.

00:53:37.836 --> 00:53:39.437
All metal and concrete.

00:53:39.437 --> 00:53:42.338
All the finishes are selected
so they don't outgas.

00:53:42.338 --> 00:53:45.071
So it doesn't foul its
own nest with pollutants.

00:53:45.071 --> 00:53:46.843
And they have actually
made these measurements.

00:53:46.843 --> 00:53:49.801
This is a slide that came from those guys.

00:53:49.801 --> 00:53:53.992
And here's the mechanical room
for this very large building.

00:53:53.992 --> 00:53:57.659
It has a tiny little
desiccant dehumidifier.

00:53:59.193 --> 00:54:00.543
This thing.

00:54:00.543 --> 00:54:01.766
There is an air handler.

00:54:01.766 --> 00:54:03.646
It's a little bit
different scale air handler

00:54:03.646 --> 00:54:05.778
as the ones I've been showing you.

00:54:05.778 --> 00:54:07.723
And it has no cooling.

00:54:07.723 --> 00:54:09.011
The building has no cooling,

00:54:09.011 --> 00:54:11.151
there is no way to cool the building.

00:54:11.151 --> 00:54:14.386
The building is cooled
because it sits on the ground.

00:54:14.386 --> 00:54:19.037
And in the winter the concrete
floor adds a little heat.

00:54:19.037 --> 00:54:22.541
And in the summer, the
concrete floor acts as a sink

00:54:22.541 --> 00:54:24.952
for heat, it actually cools the building.

00:54:24.952 --> 00:54:26.830
Now, part of the secret
of this building is

00:54:26.830 --> 00:54:29.570
it works in the Danish climate, which is

00:54:29.570 --> 00:54:32.403
cool and damp and somewhat cloudy.

00:54:33.797 --> 00:54:35.326
(laughs)

00:54:35.326 --> 00:54:38.707
I think the colors from
Twilight, you know?

00:54:38.707 --> 00:54:42.874
After they left Forks, the
Cullens moved here to Copenhagen.

00:54:44.359 --> 00:54:45.870
You gotta watch, you gotta go to movies

00:54:45.870 --> 00:54:49.563
and read teenage novels to
get that joke, but anyway.

00:54:49.563 --> 00:54:50.986
It's cold, and it's damp,

00:54:50.986 --> 00:54:52.512
but it doesn't get real cold,

00:54:52.512 --> 00:54:54.925
and it doesn't get real hot, either.

00:54:54.925 --> 00:54:57.767
And that's, you know,
that's a similar climate

00:54:57.767 --> 00:55:02.560
to much of the northwest of
the United States, by the way.

00:55:02.560 --> 00:55:05.714
And so this air handler
merely stirs the air around.

00:55:05.714 --> 00:55:07.642
There's a fan with a tiny motor,

00:55:07.642 --> 00:55:09.146
there is a heating coil in there,

00:55:09.146 --> 00:55:11.467
but it doesn't go on hardly at all.

00:55:11.467 --> 00:55:14.364
And, in fact, the heat
generated by the air dryer

00:55:14.364 --> 00:55:17.850
is enough to both heat and
dehumidify the building.

00:55:17.850 --> 00:55:20.004
And the lights are off most of the time.

00:55:20.004 --> 00:55:22.495
There's no one in there most of the time.

00:55:22.495 --> 00:55:26.386
And, like I said, it just sips energy.

00:55:26.386 --> 00:55:29.064
And here's a building that
was built along the same lines

00:55:29.064 --> 00:55:34.016
with slightly different
materials in a town called Ribe.

00:55:34.016 --> 00:55:38.384
And this, give you an idea
that's the relative humidity,

00:55:38.384 --> 00:55:40.785
here the dehumidifier failed.

00:55:40.785 --> 00:55:43.702
But it's flat 55% of the time.

00:55:44.589 --> 00:55:47.962
This is just when the dehumidifier is on.

00:55:47.962 --> 00:55:50.485
Here's the temperature
floating with the seasons,

00:55:50.485 --> 00:55:53.158
cooler in the winter,
slightly warmer but never hot

00:55:53.158 --> 00:55:55.043
in the summer.

00:55:55.043 --> 00:55:56.748
Remember, they gotta
climate that doesn't get

00:55:56.748 --> 00:55:59.207
too hot in the summer or
too cold in the winter.

00:55:59.207 --> 00:56:02.149
But there are ideas
about this that could be

00:56:02.149 --> 00:56:05.143
adapted to many places in the US.

00:56:05.143 --> 00:56:08.754
And this is the energy used
by the dehumidifier, so,

00:56:08.754 --> 00:56:12.272
a very very interesting building.

00:56:12.272 --> 00:56:13.502
Here's from their own scientists,

00:56:13.502 --> 00:56:17.548
these are the keys to
building a building like that.

00:56:17.548 --> 00:56:19.574
They say low winter
temperature is a benefit.

00:56:19.574 --> 00:56:23.229
But it doesn't get low by our standards.

00:56:23.229 --> 00:56:25.562
And that's good, because they would have

00:56:25.562 --> 00:56:28.433
to begin to add moisture if it did.

00:56:28.433 --> 00:56:30.597
Dehumidification, they find, is cheaper

00:56:30.597 --> 00:56:32.764
than humidistatic heating.

00:56:34.503 --> 00:56:35.631
The low ventilation rates,

00:56:35.631 --> 00:56:37.880
the fact that the
building is fairly tight,

00:56:37.880 --> 00:56:40.845
keeps the outdoor pollutants out.

00:56:40.845 --> 00:56:44.177
And, yet, inside it's not fouled by

00:56:44.177 --> 00:56:46.034
collection-generated pollutants.

00:56:46.034 --> 00:56:47.827
Although, you do have to pay attention

00:56:47.827 --> 00:56:50.584
to what you make the building out of.

00:56:50.584 --> 00:56:53.323
And here is the key, the
slab, the concrete floor

00:56:53.323 --> 00:56:55.908
is doing all the work of
heating and cooling it,

00:56:55.908 --> 00:57:00.504
and they've discovered
that it's important that

00:57:00.504 --> 00:57:03.537
the floor not be insulated, but the walls,

00:57:03.537 --> 00:57:08.363
the side walls and roof be
insulated very well, thermally.

00:57:08.363 --> 00:57:09.851
So we come back and look
at our own buildings,

00:57:09.851 --> 00:57:13.385
I think there's lots of
lessons and lots of opportunity

00:57:13.385 --> 00:57:16.043
for sustainability improvements.

00:57:16.043 --> 00:57:17.700
And if you wanna,

00:57:17.700 --> 00:57:19.874
last thing I wanna show you is this little

00:57:19.874 --> 00:57:22.514
things to look at to
save energy in a building

00:57:22.514 --> 00:57:26.187
like we have, and like most
of our libraries and museums.

00:57:26.187 --> 00:57:28.583
Keys are you're not gonna save money

00:57:28.583 --> 00:57:31.288
unless you undertand how the systems work.

00:57:31.288 --> 00:57:36.266
Typically, there are
low-hanging fruit opportunities.

00:57:36.266 --> 00:57:39.459
The things that consume energy are

00:57:39.459 --> 00:57:41.077
cooling the air and heating it back up

00:57:41.077 --> 00:57:42.235
when you don't have to

00:57:42.235 --> 00:57:44.873
in order to dehumidify
when you don't have to.

00:57:44.873 --> 00:57:46.998
Running your fans too much,

00:57:46.998 --> 00:57:49.998
and letting in too much outside air.

00:57:51.078 --> 00:57:53.861
Use the preservation metrics to really

00:57:53.861 --> 00:57:55.944
regulate the whole thing.

00:57:57.437 --> 00:57:58.925
Thank you very much for coming.

00:57:58.925 --> 00:58:00.865
I hope I didn't go too long.

00:58:00.865 --> 00:58:02.647
I did go too long, I'm sorry.

